      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To factor in the variability of your supplier's lead time, you can calculate the standard deviation of the lead time and use it to adjust your safety stock. This will give you a buffer for the unexpected delays. Here's a simplified way to do it:\n\n1. Calculate the average lead time (LT_avg): (14 days + 21 days) / 2 = 17.5 days\n2. Calculate the standard deviation of the lead time (σ_LT): Since we only have two data points, we'll use the range divided by 4 as an approximation. Range = 21 - 14 = 7 days, so σ_LT ≈ 7 / 4 = 1.75 days\n3. Determine your desired service level (SL) in terms of standard deviations. For a 95% service level, you might use a Z-score of 1.65 (you can find this value in a standard normal distribution table).\n4. Calculate the safety stock (SS) for lead time variability: SS = Z * σ_LT * Average Daily Demand\n\nNow, let's say your average daily demand (DD) for lithium battery cells is 100 units. Your safety stock for lead time variability would be:\n\nSS = 1.",
